{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Common Difference.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Common Difference.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"common difference common difference","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Common Difference.md","fileName":"Common Difference.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Common Difference","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Sequences and Algebraic Patterns]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Sequences and Algebraic Patterns","Arithmetic Progression","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Common Difference.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Common Difference.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"common difference common difference","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Common Difference.md","fileName":"Common Difference.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Common Difference","heading":"Common Difference","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The common difference of an [[Arithmetic Progression]] is the constant change between consecutive terms. If the terms are (a_1,a_2,a_3,\\ldots), then\n\n$$d=a_{n+1}-a_n$$\n\nhas the same value for every permitted (n). A positive difference produces an increasing progression, a negative difference a decreasing one, and zero a constant sequence. Together with the first term, the common difference determines the entire progression and its direct (n)th-term formula.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Sequences and Algebraic Patterns","Arithmetic Progression","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Common Difference.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Common Difference.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"common difference common difference","filePath":"6 